   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law, in relation to employees classified as\nthe Triborough bridge and tunnel authority superior officers benevolent\nassociation of an MTA-employer and the resolution of disputes in the course of\ncollective negotiations",
    "basePrintNo": "S4897",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4897-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 50,
       "fullName": "John Mannion",
       "imgName": "1315_John_Mannion.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1498,
       "sessionMemberId": 1854,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "MANNION"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §209, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4897\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law, in relation to employees classi-\nfied as the Triborough bridge and tunnel authority superior officers\nbenevolent association of an MTA-employer and the resolution of disputes\nin the course of collective negotiations\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow certified or registered public employee organizations to repre-\nsent employees as the Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority Superior\nOfficer Benevolent Association of an MTA-public employer and to be\ncovered by the provisions of section 209 of the Civil Service Law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends section 209 of the Civil Service Law to grant binding\narbitration to Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority Superior Officer\nBenevolent Association members.\n\nSection two states that this bill shall take effect immediately,\nprovided, however, that the amendments to paragraph (f) of section\n209(5) of the Civil Service Law made by section one of this bill shall\nnot affect the expiration of such subdivision and shall be deemed to\nexpire therewith.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent provisions of Chapter 929 of the Laws of 1986 relating to\ncompulsory arbitration to resolve impasses in collective negotiations\nbetween an MTA public employer and a public employee organization left\nout certain employees from receiving this benefit. This bill would\ncorrect this inequity.\n\nOver the years, compulsory arbitration has allowed the people of New\nYork to enjoy uninterrupted services. All evaluations of the effects of\nthis law have been positive and have advocated the continuation of this\nprocess.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: S3918 - Vetoed, Memo No. 198\n\n2018: S.5815 - Vetoed, Memo No. 283\n\n2017: S.5815 - 3rd Reading Calendar\n\n2015: S.3524 - Vetoed, No. 204\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately, provided, however, that the\namendments to paragraph (f) of section 209(5) of the Civil Service Law\nmade by section one of this bill shall not affect the expiration of such\nsubdivision and shall be deemed to expire therewith.\n",
